WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.820
World-class track meet.

00:00:02.180 --> 00:00:05.100
Run, jump, and race to your heart's content.

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:28.300
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:29.320
I'm Mike.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:30.280
And I'm Sean.

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:31.720
And that's all for today.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:34.440
That's it, really. Seriously, we're going to end the podcast right now.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:37.500
Good night. What I mean is that it's just me and Sean today.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:43.240
It's a nice intimate record because this game, as some of you know, isn't really that important.

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:45.620
So others have abandoned us.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:49.260
Yeah, it's basically we're getting a duplicate.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:54.980
We accidentally played the same game twice, but it turns out that they just released it twice.

00:00:54.980 --> 00:01:02.520
Yeah, this one's this one's strange because the person who releases it changes to like to make a long story short here.

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:08.060
World-class track meet is the way almost everyone who grew up with an NES knows this game.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:13.780
And you know it fairly well because it also got packaged in later on with Super Mario Brothers and Duck Hunt.

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:22.200
So a lot of late NES buyers, if you bought it around like 90 or 91, you probably got this as like the sole pack-in game, which is great.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:24.520
I mean, three games on one card is pretty cool.

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:25.580
The value.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:30.200
Yeah. Well, I mean, I don't even know what an NES would be going for in 1990.

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:32.460
It'd probably come with everything, too.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.100
You know, the power pad, the zapper, hopefully not Rob.

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:37.100
Stickers, probably.

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:38.700
Yeah. Stickers are cool.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:41.960
Yeah, I like stickers. Where do you stand on the whole like putting stickers on your console?

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:43.940
That's stupid. But as a kid?

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:45.580
I wouldn't have done it.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:46.840
Okay. I didn't do it either.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:52.360
Yeah. I think I one time put stickers on my Guitar Hero guitar, and even I regretted that.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:54.160
I said, what am I going to do? And I was able to trade it.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:02:03.120
So that was great. To get back to the point, World Class Track Meet is better known as stadium events in the chronologicalness of everything.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:05.020
We've already played stadium events.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:09.680
So you're wrong if you thought this was track and field now turning into World Class Track Meet.

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:14.900
This is stadium events now being bought out by Nintendo from Bandai.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:20.580
You remember Bandai had the Family Fun Fitness Pad, and Nintendo said, hey, that sounds great.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:27.320
We'd like to buy that and all of your games, and we'll release something called the Power Pad.

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:31.380
Yeah. So we know all about the Power Pad.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:37.960
We've talked about like at least, you know, we were talking about the old version of this, but it's basically the same thing.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:42.200
Do we want to go through like the essential games list for this now?

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:43.260
I think we will.

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:46.280
One quick thing, though, since we're talking about the Power Pad.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:47.860
Do you know why they called it the Power Pad?

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:50.440
To go along with the Power Glove.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:51.740
Power Glove was later.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:54.780
You know, they had plans for the Power Glove.

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:59.960
Okay, cool. I think it had a lot to do with that now you're playing with power tagline.

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:01.260
Now you're cooking with gas.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:08.880
So I think it's like, you know, yeah, that'd be funny if their tagline was now you're cooking with gas and they called it the gas pad.

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:10.640
I like that.

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:16.400
Yeah, no, we will do the essential games list very shortly, but there was just one immediate problem that I had about this thing.

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:18.760
And something didn't sit right with me, okay?

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:26.320
If 23,000 copies of stadium events were allegedly sold before it was like taken off the market, right?

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:28.460
Mm-hmm. And that's all alleged.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:29.600
We will never know how many.

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:32.900
Some people think it's as low as like 1,000 copies were actually sold.

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:42.080
But even if 23,000 sold, there is a real problem here that some lesser informed person could have bought the same game twice.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:51.260
They could have already owned stadium events, liked it so much, and then said, wow, I like games with pads so much I'm going to buy world-class track meet.

00:03:51.260 --> 00:04:04.080
And then they find out it's the same game because it literally, outside of a repaint on the title screen, even the way that the selection for the different modes, they're all placed out the same way using the same text.

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:05.300
So it's just a logo swap.

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:13.840
Honestly, like the actual circumstance is different, but this happens with me today.

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:20.400
Like just with the fact that, you know, I own a game on some service.

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:26.180
Like maybe I just bought a Humble Bundle, didn't notice I already owned it, and I buy it again on sale on Steam.

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:27.820
Like this happens to me all the time.

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:30.880
Right, right, right. But with different circumstances surrounding it.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:33.020
So, you know, it's a problem as old as time.

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:41.240
And we've touched a lot on the stadium events episode about rarity of games and what video games are worth and collecting video games.

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:56.520
But I wanted to add to that here because now that World Class Track Meet exists, we should talk about is there anything actually like worth it of stadium events and the fact that like World Class Track Meet sold millions of copies.

00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:07.200
So it's kind of weird that just because a game is packaged differently that it sells for an incredible more amount of money when you're virtually playing the same programmed game.

00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:15.340
Yeah. I mean, I'm not a collector aside from like some things that I just think look cool, I guess.

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:24.080
But, you know, if you see a game that's got the PlayStation that's got that green greatest hits, it doesn't quite look as cool, I guess.

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:28.000
Or obviously it doesn't carry as much market value as the original Black.

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:31.320
So, I mean, it's just market forces.

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:32.760
I don't know. No, it's true.

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:39.080
I mean, there's a demand for it because, you know, as I was talking about that, I was kind of thinking like, well, I'm kind of talking about collector's editions too, right?

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:43.480
Like, why would a collector's edition be worth more than a normal version if you're just playing the same game?

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:45.840
Sometimes it's about the packaging more than anything.

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:59.480
So, I don't think when collecting video games, I actually feel like that whole market has very little to do with like love of video games and more to do with just this like obsession of completion.

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:10.280
Yeah. You know, you want it, you want the things, especially the things that are in higher demand, like say stadium events because there's only so many known copies of it.

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:16.840
You want them because only so many few people can actually complete the collection because of its availability.

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:26.180
Exactly. And it's the same as, you know, the conspiracy theory that says that all modern art is just a conspiracy by the mega rich to consolidate all their money.

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:28.620
Yeah, that's true. Do they get taxed on that?

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:29.860
I have no idea.

00:06:30.140 --> 00:06:35.520
Yeah, but it's a good way to move funds into art because like, I feel like they don't get taxed on that.

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:37.400
And then, and then they.

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:40.780
I don't know. I'd have to, I'd have to read up some stuff about Sotheby's.

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:41.880
I'm not sure. Got it.

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:43.520
Yeah. Sotheby's, they're behind this whole thing.

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:44.640
The whole shebang.

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:48.040
And if we're going to talk about auctions, we might as well talk about it's over now.

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:56.840
But at the time of this record, there are three days left on the Nintendo PlayStation auction for Heritage.

00:06:57.940 --> 00:07:01.440
Heritage auction. Put it up like a month or two ago.

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:03.540
No one knew what it was going to sell for.

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:06.740
You know now if you just look it up and I'll post it in the show notes here.

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:14.280
But right now it's sitting at like $228,000, which is more like upwards of $300,000 after like all the fees and stuff.

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:16.620
Yeah. What do you think this thing's going to finally hit?

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:23.760
I mean, I'm not sure how auctions mutate over time.

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:28.080
I don't know if there's like a huge uptick in the last few minutes.

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:29.800
I imagine they would.

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:33.080
So if that's the case. I think that happens especially for online auctions there too.

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:36.280
I would say maybe it'll hit half a mil.

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:39.240
This is a random guess.

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:40.980
Yeah. And that's what they're all about.

00:07:41.140 --> 00:07:45.640
You know, random guesses. First off, for those of you who have no idea what I'm talking about, you're like, you just said two companies.

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:47.480
No, I didn't because I never said Sony.

00:07:48.460 --> 00:07:54.940
Nintendo PlayStation was the like add-on successor to the Super Nintendo that was going to be able to play CDs.

00:07:56.060 --> 00:07:58.000
And then Sony took their ball and left.

00:07:58.220 --> 00:08:01.340
Yeah. Basically, Nintendo was like, no, we're actually cool without CDs.

00:08:01.340 --> 00:08:05.180
We got this new flash disk format for N64.

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:07.060
And Sony's like, what the heck?

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:07.940
We'll just make our own.

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:09.400
We already have the software set up.

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:10.940
So they released the PlayStation instead.

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:14.880
One of these was found in the wild and is now at auction.

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:18.520
Before anybody yells about this should be preserved, it has been preserved.

00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:19.700
The BIOS is dumped.

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:21.380
There's no games for it.

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:22.300
So you don't have to worry.

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:25.400
Yeah. What? You don't have to worry about that anymore.

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:31.960
I initially guessed that this would easily be the highest selling video game thing of all time.

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:33.560
But that's not much.

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:35.560
That's like $100,000, right?

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:38.400
I could have sworn there was something that just went for over a million.

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:40.500
Wasn't there? Over a million?

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:42.980
I don't know. I guess I'm just talking on my ass.

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:47.080
Yeah. I mean, I'm trying to think, but I can't think of anything that went for over a million.

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:55.880
I believe a brand new copy, like a sealed copy of Super Mario Brothers was bought by some art collector for like $100,000.

00:08:56.140 --> 00:08:59.940
Because they don't get it that that thing is not necessarily in demand.

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:05.700
Yeah. But as time goes on, Super Mario Brothers is going to be like the coveted video game.

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:09.920
And to have it completely sealed is worth some kind of art.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:15.920
We don't really know what video game art is right now in like the same way that, you know, when they were doing the Renaissance,

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:21.360
they didn't think like, oh, this thing's going to be selling for, you know, billions of what our dollars are currently worth.

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:25.500
I must be thinking more about the comic book world because that gets up there.

00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:26.700
The comic book world right now.

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:28.540
Yeah. It's like Superman.

00:09:29.340 --> 00:09:34.420
Well, Action Comics number one, the first appearance of Superman is close to like 3 million.

00:09:34.660 --> 00:09:41.260
Okay. Yeah. And I'm pretty sure if another copy of like Detective Comics 27, which is the first appearance of Batman,

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:49.200
if that went up for auction now at like a 9.8 or something like that, which would be unheard of, that would easily go over 3 million.

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:55.880
And now before we entirely move on from this sidebar, that also sort of raises a question.

00:09:55.880 --> 00:10:01.040
Yeah. I think that's a question because, you know, with physical material, you can restore it.

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:06.980
Like, you know, I don't know what the process is for, you know, the pulp paper that a comic book was written on.

00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:08.580
It's very easy to restore it.

00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:12.940
You can restore it and you can restore paintings and you can restore statues.

00:10:12.940 --> 00:10:20.140
But I don't really know what happens when the chip in a Nintendo game starts to degrade.

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:29.400
Like, I know that we have it all digitally preserved, but will these physical boxes mean anything if the actual chip inside is degraded?

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:30.880
Yeah. No, it's very true.

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:38.700
Does it become like a form of art that the cartridge itself has the logo of the game that used to work, printed more money on it?

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:51.780
You know, this was kind of experienced in the early 2000s when the – and it didn't happen to all NESs, but the pins, the contact pins on the actual console themselves started to decay.

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:53.900
And they wouldn't read the games properly.

00:10:53.900 --> 00:10:56.120
And everybody thought like, oh, no, our NESs are broken.

00:10:56.120 --> 00:11:03.960
But what they figured out is just to insert a new 52 pin to the NES and that would get the cartridges reading like normal.

00:11:04.360 --> 00:11:06.200
But that's – what's that?

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:07.680
Yeah. Right, right, right.

00:11:07.820 --> 00:11:08.760
It becomes a new NES.

00:11:08.840 --> 00:11:13.080
But also, it's not the same thing because all you did was change your NES.

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:16.540
That's like just saying that you don't have an original Nintendo Entertainment System.

00:11:16.780 --> 00:11:19.860
It's not changing the cartridge itself.

00:11:20.020 --> 00:11:21.420
So I think that's a great question.

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:31.820
Yeah. I think it would have to be handled in a weird way similar to how comic books and art handle it too where you have a person who detects for restoration.

00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:37.160
And if it is been – if like Action Comics number one comes in at like CGC 9.5, right?

00:11:37.420 --> 00:11:40.360
And they do some – well, you can't get a 9.5.

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:41.820
You can only – you get a 9.6.

00:11:42.100 --> 00:11:42.920
Sorry about that.

00:11:43.880 --> 00:11:48.280
They would give it a purple label on the CGC to note that it's restored.

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:54.540
And that would bring it down like several hundred thousand dollars because of that note that it's been restored.

00:11:54.680 --> 00:11:56.760
And it also depends on the kind of restoration too.

00:11:56.940 --> 00:12:02.760
Like did you take a cover from a different Action Comics number one or did you just like – did you press it?

00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:07.620
You know, sometimes presses are so good you can't even tell and they don't get listed on the restored.

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:10.760
And then you have to wonder, has mine been restored or has it not?

00:12:10.760 --> 00:12:12.800
But – so those are all things to consider.

00:12:12.920 --> 00:12:23.720
But maybe there's like a thing for that for video game collectors too where they – like they get separated between like working copy and like this was at one time a working copy.

00:12:23.780 --> 00:12:26.480
And it's just like a pile of mush now.

00:12:26.660 --> 00:12:28.580
Yeah. I mean it's a really scary thing to think about.

00:12:28.700 --> 00:12:34.220
But thankfully the real fight in video game preservation is mostly done.

00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:42.880
Yeah. You know, a lot of the games that mean something throughout time are readily available for you.

00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:53.960
It's when it gets to stuff like the business side where like Nintendo doesn't want anyone to know what happened at their company ever other than the current thing that they're working on.

00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:56.940
That stuff's all getting lost unless someone preserves it.

00:12:57.480 --> 00:13:03.220
Prototypes of games, that stuff's hard to find because they don't like – they don't have readily available.

00:13:03.220 --> 00:13:08.260
Like it's really cool if you see someone collecting Sonic prototypes because there's like a whole community for that.

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:11.920
You can play like six different prototypes of Sonic the Hedgehog 3.

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:13.980
But you have to imagine that they were making that game.

00:13:14.100 --> 00:13:16.320
They went through like hundreds of versions, right?

00:13:16.480 --> 00:13:19.880
Like they must have had hundreds of working copies with little bug fixes here and there.

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:21.600
It's going to be really hard to find all that stuff.

00:13:21.900 --> 00:13:23.260
Who's that for? I don't know.

00:13:23.340 --> 00:13:28.880
But the fact that it can easily be preserved for kilobytes means that we should be trying to do that stuff.

00:13:28.880 --> 00:13:32.860
No one should be worried about how are we going to play Super Mario Brothers in 2100.

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:34.420
Like someone will find a way.

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:35.880
It's not going to be that hard.

00:13:36.040 --> 00:13:39.300
But how are we going to play Destiny 2 in 2100?

00:13:39.560 --> 00:13:42.260
Right. We probably will be able to.

00:13:43.400 --> 00:13:45.600
Similar to how like people are still playing Final Fantasy 11.

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:47.620
That just blows my mind every single day.

00:13:48.320 --> 00:13:51.480
It's like there's that Final Fantasy 14 is like apparently really good.

00:13:51.660 --> 00:13:59.300
So many people play that. And then there's also this other community of people playing a decade older MMORPG that still gets updates.

