And they like what you have to say.

00:16:26.360 --> 00:16:31.500
You have a very focused point of view, if I may say so.

00:16:31.700 --> 00:16:37.220
Oh, thanks, Mike. So first, I just want to get on the topic of accessories for the NES.

00:16:37.740 --> 00:16:41.640
Okay. The power pad was the focus of World Class Track Meet

00:16:41.640 --> 00:16:44.060
and the Family Fun Fitness pad before that.

00:16:44.300 --> 00:16:47.400
Nintendo released with a little thing called Rob.

00:16:47.980 --> 00:16:53.500
Oh, yeah. We haven't talked about Rob since the second time he ever released a game

00:16:53.500 --> 00:16:55.020
and then went away for good.

00:16:55.520 --> 00:16:59.700
What do you think is like on if you're like a kid in 1988?

00:17:00.020 --> 00:17:04.780
Just remember, people are just now starting to own Nintendo Entertainment Systems

00:17:04.780 --> 00:17:06.180
and they're getting their Rob.

00:17:06.700 --> 00:17:09.800
They know that there hasn't been a game out for Rob,

00:17:09.880 --> 00:17:12.360
or they don't for the last three years.

00:17:14.080 --> 00:17:15.780
What's going on with that?

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:17.580
Do you throw that out at this point?

00:17:17.700 --> 00:17:18.660
Are you done with Rob? No.

00:17:19.240 --> 00:17:25.120
Okay. So when I was a kid, there was a remote control robot called Rad.

00:17:25.660 --> 00:17:26.720
Like R-A-D.

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:27.820
I had it. Yeah.

00:17:29.480 --> 00:17:33.960
I had that in my room up until I moved out.

00:17:34.800 --> 00:17:36.520
It was just buried under stuff.

00:17:36.660 --> 00:17:43.680
Yeah, yeah. And so I mean, at least me, like even with more recent examples,

00:17:43.900 --> 00:17:47.660
like the iToy, like that was just in a cupboard somewhere.

00:17:47.840 --> 00:17:49.840
I never throw anything like that out.

00:17:49.980 --> 00:17:54.940
Got it. Because it is a novelty item and you never know when you might need it.

00:17:55.580 --> 00:18:00.940
The iToy is a great example because that's even like a little more of a niche thing.

00:18:01.060 --> 00:18:03.140
I was thinking right away with defunct hardware,

00:18:03.140 --> 00:18:05.720
I think of all the rock band and Guitar Hero gear.

00:18:05.720 --> 00:18:11.720
Yeah. Like everybody has this plastic drum set somewhere in their house that they don't use.

00:18:11.860 --> 00:18:13.660
Like nobody threw it out, but it's just there.

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:14.920
I want to play it again.

00:18:14.920 --> 00:18:17.440
But I would never throw that stuff out either.

00:18:17.640 --> 00:18:20.180
Like that's just how I look at peripherals.

00:18:20.240 --> 00:18:26.060
And no matter how unwieldy they are, I think that if I bought it, I'm going to keep it.

00:18:26.620 --> 00:18:28.400
So that's how I stand on it.

00:18:28.960 --> 00:18:34.920
And true to form, I am somehow the same way because even though I don't have anything in my apartment,

00:18:35.480 --> 00:18:40.440
back at my parents' house, I kept everything in terms of defunct hardware.

00:18:40.700 --> 00:18:45.260
Things that for consoles that I've just like bought but never bought any games for.

00:18:45.380 --> 00:18:46.280
Even have stuff like that.

00:18:46.460 --> 00:18:48.320
I have Game Gear that I own nothing for.

00:18:48.460 --> 00:18:50.600
Right. It doesn't make any sense.

00:18:50.720 --> 00:18:52.740
But you never know when you're going to need that stuff.

00:18:52.880 --> 00:18:54.040
And that's a hoarder mentality.

00:18:54.280 --> 00:18:57.700
I know. And I don't think anyone should feel bad if they throw out their defunct hardware.

00:18:57.960 --> 00:19:06.840
But I'm going to take a good guess that anybody who bought Rob still has it in 1988 on the off chance that they might make another game for Rob.

00:19:06.920 --> 00:19:08.740
Remember, they never canceled Rob.

00:19:08.880 --> 00:19:12.540
They never said like he's out of print or we're not.

00:19:12.540 --> 00:19:13.480
Rob is canceled.

00:19:13.480 --> 00:19:15.000
Right, right. Rob is canceled.

00:19:15.180 --> 00:19:17.880
Rob's dead. They never did anything with that.

00:19:17.880 --> 00:19:19.440
And I think that's just a little strange.

00:19:19.900 --> 00:19:25.340
I think I would have felt some ill will towards Nintendo like what happened to him.

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:28.280
Because the Zapper is getting continuous releases.

00:19:28.520 --> 00:19:32.240
Yeah. And the Power Pad is – it will get some stuff.

00:19:32.420 --> 00:19:37.080
Yeah. But I wouldn't be angry per se.

00:19:37.220 --> 00:19:39.480
Especially because – could you buy it by itself?

00:19:39.480 --> 00:19:42.380
Yeah. Apparently you could.

00:19:42.640 --> 00:19:48.360
Ooh. But I don't know – like I don't have any images to verify it of like it at a retail store.

00:19:48.600 --> 00:19:51.380
Yeah. It usually came packaged with the NES.

00:19:51.700 --> 00:19:52.880
I mean if it just came with it –

00:19:52.880 --> 00:19:58.360
Or maybe it came with Gyromite. I mean if it just came with it then I really couldn't feel bad about it.

00:19:58.440 --> 00:20:03.220
But if I went and I bought it because I thought this was going to be the future of video games –

00:20:03.220 --> 00:20:07.440
Right. I would have some doubts about my judgment but also be a little more pissed off.

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:16.440
And the other accessories to keep in mind right now that are actually useful is something we referenced on the show before, the NES Advantage.

00:20:16.540 --> 00:20:25.100
That is a giant like square block that then has an actual joystick and arcade-like buttons.

00:20:25.320 --> 00:20:31.720
You know like the size of – a little bit bigger than a quarter but they feel like a normal arcade press and stuff like that.

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:36.920
That was designed to be like for pro players because remember pro players went to the arcade.

00:20:36.920 --> 00:20:39.000
Yeah. So if you really wanted to step up –

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:40.160
And that's still a thing. Yeah.

00:20:40.280 --> 00:20:45.000
It is. I mean you see that now even with the Xbox – what do they have?

00:20:45.140 --> 00:20:50.100
They have the Xbox One X regular controller but then they also have the Elite controller I think it's called.

00:20:50.580 --> 00:20:56.280
Well I was more looking into like the USB arcade –

00:20:56.280 --> 00:20:57.500
Oh like fight sticks.

00:20:57.660 --> 00:20:58.480
Fight sticks. Yes.

00:20:58.960 --> 00:21:11.080
Okay. Right. Now the problem with the NES Advantage that I am thinking about is that as of right now outside of maybe the shmups there's no real like use for that joystick.

00:21:11.820 --> 00:21:22.240
I mean looking back on how often we've played games that are just so arcade – that they show their roots in the arcade so heavily.

00:21:22.460 --> 00:21:28.180
Like I imagine that almost any game on the NES feels a bit more comfortable on this thing.

00:21:28.320 --> 00:21:31.460
But they still only read the same direction that a D-pad reads.

00:21:31.600 --> 00:21:32.860
It's not like you can –

00:21:32.860 --> 00:21:38.340
A joystick does have a very different tactile response than as a D-pad.

00:21:38.620 --> 00:21:40.360
So I don't know.

00:21:40.500 --> 00:21:44.860
I think I would maybe enjoy some of these games more if I was using a joystick.

00:21:45.180 --> 00:21:51.300
Yeah. And I don't know if you play on a traditional NES controller but that's what I do.

00:21:51.400 --> 00:21:53.480
And sometimes I do wish the buttons were bigger.

00:21:54.340 --> 00:22:03.760
So I think that that would have been nice too to have like a full separate arcade feel for each button as well as having a dedicated hand to your –

00:22:03.760 --> 00:22:05.260
You know, I just feel like the way that I hold –

00:22:05.260 --> 00:22:10.120
The way that I hold the controller now is very based on the DualShock and the Xbox One controllers.

00:22:10.600 --> 00:22:12.800
So my index fingers are like arched.

00:22:12.900 --> 00:22:13.780
I'm showing Sean right now.

00:22:13.840 --> 00:22:15.080
But you guys know what I'm talking about.

00:22:15.140 --> 00:22:17.600
Like where the L1 and R1 would be.

00:22:17.760 --> 00:22:23.520
They're like that. And so I don't know how people used to play NES games and stuff like that.

00:22:23.640 --> 00:22:28.520
Like I don't know if they held the controller with like their – all the rest of their fingers tucked behind the controller.

00:22:28.720 --> 00:22:35.200
But I do the same thing and tuck in the index fingers on the top of the square box.

00:22:35.220 --> 00:22:39.600
You know what? I've done some research into 80s movies and I just –

00:22:39.600 --> 00:22:45.760
I look at the scenes in which they're playing video games and they seem to just hold it like a remote control with two hands.

00:22:46.020 --> 00:22:47.080
Got it. Okay. Yeah.

00:22:47.080 --> 00:22:54.260
And if you would look into our next accessory here, if you would look up the NES Max, that comes out in 1988.

00:22:54.280 --> 00:22:57.340
The exact date isn't known so I can't say if it's already out or not.

00:22:57.640 --> 00:23:00.220
But this takes on the shape of a more familiar controller.

00:23:00.480 --> 00:23:03.900
But you never really see this in any promotional work or anything.

00:23:04.140 --> 00:23:06.460
Kind of looks like a mistake.

00:23:07.080 --> 00:23:09.380
Yeah. Kind of looks like a mistake.

00:23:09.540 --> 00:23:13.540
I actually thought it could be a little more comfortable than the traditional NES controller.

00:23:14.160 --> 00:23:18.540
It looks like it at least has the attempt at ergonomic design.

00:23:19.120 --> 00:23:21.560
But it just – those curves.

00:23:21.560 --> 00:23:26.100
If you're looking at it yourself, listener, the curves just look wrong.

00:23:27.600 --> 00:23:29.660
Because they're too jagged?

00:23:29.960 --> 00:23:32.320
Is it the – like they're too blocky?

00:23:32.640 --> 00:23:42.260
It's like when you try to – if you're not an artist and you try to do like a freehand circle, like compare that to like an actual circle.

00:23:42.480 --> 00:23:43.580
That's what I like.

00:23:43.680 --> 00:23:50.680
Somebody tried to draw the idea of a controller, but they tried to do like a freehand circle.

00:23:50.680 --> 00:23:52.200
I don't know how I'm describing this.

00:23:52.400 --> 00:23:55.760
I understand. And also, I kind of like this joystick better.

00:23:55.900 --> 00:23:58.060
I'm actually surprised to see this that far back.

00:23:58.660 --> 00:23:59.520
It looks –

00:23:59.520 --> 00:24:01.540
It's not as pronounced as an arcade joystick.

00:24:01.540 --> 00:24:06.300
It looks more like something on the GameCube.

00:24:06.420 --> 00:24:08.080
You know, like that GameCube analog.

00:24:08.440 --> 00:24:11.200
See, I was looking at some very low-res images.

00:24:11.200 --> 00:24:13.580
I thought it was just a red D-pad, but you're right.

00:24:13.580 --> 00:24:14.580
That is a joystick.

00:24:14.580 --> 00:24:23.820
Yeah, if you look at one top down, it will look like a terrible like thumbprint D-pad, but it's actually a joystick.

00:24:24.120 --> 00:24:26.660
So it sort of looks like what's on the 3DS.

00:24:27.360 --> 00:24:28.360
Yeah, that's true.

00:24:28.560 --> 00:24:33.040
Yeah, the 3DS was a little more strict in movement.

00:24:33.400 --> 00:24:35.280
This one has a little more wobble to it.

00:24:35.440 --> 00:24:39.300
Wibble. The weebles wobble, but they don't fall down.

00:24:40.540 --> 00:24:43.780
Yeah, so I really like this controller, and I do want to get my hands on this.

00:24:43.780 --> 00:24:46.040
I also like that it has a turbo option.

00:24:46.260 --> 00:24:54.700
Not that the NES Advantage had that as well, but this one seems in better proximity to the bottom of your thumbs, which is what I use for the B&A.

00:24:54.820 --> 00:24:55.820
I just switch off between them.

00:24:56.200 --> 00:24:57.240
I only ever use one thumb.

00:24:57.400 --> 00:24:58.680
I should make that note. That's good.

00:24:59.120 --> 00:25:03.280
I don't play with the controller down on a table and use my –

00:25:03.280 --> 00:25:05.080
Hey, if you have to, then that's fine too, though.

00:25:05.360 --> 00:25:09.820
I mean, I think some people actually do that for like world record strategies and stuff like that.

00:25:09.820 --> 00:25:15.080
Yeah, and if you are looking for this controller, I mean, looking at it right now, it looks like it's only 18 bucks.

00:25:15.280 --> 00:25:20.800
Oh, great. Okay, so I can pick that up because we just made a million dollars on the podcast last month.

00:25:21.080 --> 00:25:22.600
That's true. So shout out to all the donors.

00:25:23.180 --> 00:25:25.260
I think that's disingenuous, Mike.

00:25:25.320 --> 00:25:28.020
Okay. That's probably true.

00:25:28.200 --> 00:25:29.040
We didn't make a million.

00:25:29.260 --> 00:25:30.180
We made a billion.

00:25:31.900 --> 00:25:36.600
We made a billion dollars, and we're going to open – we're going to re – basically, we've talked to Nintendo.

00:25:36.600 --> 00:25:39.540
We're going to open up the factories again and print more NESs.

00:25:39.740 --> 00:25:43.660
We're also going to put up a paywall around the podcast, so see you later.

00:25:43.860 --> 00:25:45.760
Do we need a paywall if we made a billion dollars?

00:25:46.120 --> 00:25:47.400
We want more money.

00:25:50.840 --> 00:25:53.240
All right, so that's it for Nintendo for a moment.

00:25:53.240 --> 00:25:54.280
We'll come back to them.

00:25:54.420 --> 00:25:56.220
I want to tell you what's going on at the arcade.

00:25:56.400 --> 00:26:00.200
What's happening? Because I want to know what – where are our ports?

00:26:00.560 --> 00:26:03.680
We have gotten a lot of great arcade games, right?

00:26:03.680 --> 00:26:08.220
Mm-hmm. Port it over to the NES, but we are missing some really, really good stuff, okay?

00:26:08.420 --> 00:26:09.660
We're missing Pac-Man.

00:26:10.320 --> 00:26:11.440
Okay? True. From 1980.

00:26:12.220 --> 00:26:15.520
Yeah. Why can't Pac-Man be on the NES at this point?

00:26:15.640 --> 00:26:20.220
I mean, I've looked at the other home console ports of Pac-Man, and I don't think I want that.

00:26:20.500 --> 00:26:25.320
Okay. If that's what they're going to do with it, like the Atari 2600 version, I'm sure it could be done a little bit better on the NES,

00:26:25.320 --> 00:26:31.300
but there's – I don't know what kind of processing power is going into that Pac-Man.

00:26:31.480 --> 00:26:32.840
Yeah, yeah. Okay, I understand.

00:26:33.040 --> 00:26:34.180
But it is something –

00:26:34.180 --> 00:26:35.840
But it's advanced. Yeah, it's so advanced.

00:26:35.860 --> 00:26:39.020
I don't want to have that experience with Pac-Man.

00:26:39.200 --> 00:26:40.100
All right, here's the next one.

00:26:40.360 --> 00:26:41.380
Street Fighter 1.

00:26:42.360 --> 00:26:45.020
Capcom is very loyal to the NES.

00:26:45.020 --> 00:26:46.420
That is strange. Why isn't this getting a port?

00:26:46.420 --> 00:26:47.960
When did Street Fighter 1 come out?

00:26:48.080 --> 00:26:48.880
That was 87.

00:26:49.560 --> 00:26:50.520
Really? Yes.

00:26:50.520 --> 00:26:53.260
Okay, so we had the proto Street Fighter.

00:26:53.540 --> 00:26:56.460
Yes, we did. And we still haven't gotten – I mean, it's only been a year.

00:26:56.620 --> 00:26:57.740
Do we ever get Street Fighter 1?

00:26:57.900 --> 00:26:58.940
Well, I don't think we ever do.

00:26:59.140 --> 00:27:01.600
We don't ever. We get Street Fighter 2010, I think.

00:27:02.860 --> 00:27:03.660
So what?

00:27:03.960 --> 00:27:05.460
You don't know about Street Fighter 2010?

00:27:05.600 --> 00:27:08.860
I've never heard of it. It sounds like you're talking about Street Fighter 4 or something.

00:27:08.860 --> 00:27:14.260
Yeah, I almost don't even want to spoil it for you because it's like one of those things Capcom doesn't want anyone to know about.

00:27:14.420 --> 00:27:15.740
So when do we get to play that?

00:27:16.820 --> 00:27:19.140
That we get to play in 1990.

00:27:19.760 --> 00:27:21.380
Yikes. So we're two years away from that.

00:27:21.560 --> 00:27:24.880
And we'll never see Street Fighter on the NES.

00:27:25.040 --> 00:27:26.060
So, yeah, I don't know.

00:27:26.340 --> 00:27:28.020
I mean, I thought this was –

00:27:28.020 --> 00:27:33.840
That one, I think, especially knowing that, like, maybe they couldn't program the two-player mode into it.

00:27:33.860 --> 00:27:38.440
And that was like, well, that's the whole point of having the game is to fight against friends, right?

00:27:38.540 --> 00:27:40.080
Like that kind of combat system.

00:27:40.260 --> 00:27:43.180
So maybe they were just – they were like, yeah, whatever.

00:27:43.460 --> 00:27:44.340
You know, we'll figure this out.

00:27:45.100 --> 00:27:49.900
Two games that I'm pretty sure we're going to get on the NES but have not come out yet.

00:27:50.080 --> 00:27:50.960
But they are out.

00:27:51.300 --> 00:27:52.500
First one is R-Type.

00:27:52.860 --> 00:27:54.300
Okay? Famous shmup series.

00:27:54.900 --> 00:27:56.260
I think we're going to get that.

00:27:56.700 --> 00:27:58.980
No, no, no. That was Z – Sector Z.

00:27:59.120 --> 00:28:02.480
Sector Z. And all the Konami shmups.

00:28:02.700 --> 00:28:04.260
But R-Type is a different one.

00:28:04.340 --> 00:28:06.100
And I'm pretty sure we get that on the NES.

00:28:06.300 --> 00:28:09.300
But if we don't, I'll be upset.

00:28:11.440 --> 00:28:17.140
But I guess what I meant to say is that we're getting so many shmup games, you would think that this would just be a natural fit if it's doing well in the arcade.

00:28:17.840 --> 00:28:35.040
True. You know, I guess I just don't know what their thought process is as to like – like maybe the idea of expanding to the home market isn't as widely thought of as a good idea for some companies.

00:28:35.180 --> 00:28:37.300
But like is – who made R-Type?

00:28:37.460 --> 00:28:38.940
R-Type is made by Irem.

00:28:39.420 --> 00:28:41.160
So, yeah. They should know better.

00:28:42.000 --> 00:28:45.520
And the official word is that R-Type will not come to the NES.

00:28:45.840 --> 00:28:46.760
We will not see it.

00:28:46.940 --> 00:28:48.260
Are we sure? Yes.

00:28:48.440 --> 00:28:50.080
That's what I have.

00:28:50.160 --> 00:28:52.940
Not in the future. I've just checked the catalog and know.

00:28:53.580 --> 00:29:01.760
Apparently in like 2015 someone did make a like homebrew R-Type for NES that does work.

00:29:01.900 --> 00:29:03.620
And honestly, I'll link the video.

00:29:03.740 --> 00:29:04.620
It looks pretty cool.

00:29:04.800 --> 00:29:06.760
Okay. So, it's got that going on for it.

00:29:06.800 --> 00:29:08.100
I'm like, that's something.

00:29:08.340 --> 00:29:12.680
I don't know how realistic it is to the arcade game because I never played that.

00:29:12.800 --> 00:29:14.160
I played the later R-Types.

00:29:14.540 --> 00:29:15.660
I never played any of them.

00:29:15.860 --> 00:29:17.320
Interesting. Okay. R-Type is cool.

00:29:17.320 --> 00:29:22.540
It's like Contra Meets.

00:29:23.320 --> 00:29:24.560
Interesting. Okay.

00:29:24.840 --> 00:29:26.940
Because the alien thing is much more pronounced.

00:29:27.540 --> 00:29:28.380
Huh. Okay.

00:29:29.040 --> 00:29:34.400
And the other game I was going to mention that this one I know comes to the NES in a different

00:29:34.400 --> 00:29:39.260
way. Sid Meier's Pirates is out not in the arcades but on PC.

00:29:40.280 --> 00:29:41.320
That's insane.

00:29:41.320 --> 00:29:43.460
And I can't wait for that one.

00:29:43.800 --> 00:29:45.780
But it's not here yet.

00:29:46.320 --> 00:29:47.480
So... Is that another 1990?

00:29:47.920 --> 00:29:49.280
I feel like that's late.

00:29:49.500 --> 00:29:51.240
Yeah. Just because of...

00:29:51.240 --> 00:29:52.960
You know, but then that begs the question too.

00:29:53.240 --> 00:29:58.660
For Sid Meier's Pirates to make it to the NES but for Street Fighter 1 not to be able

00:29:58.660 --> 00:30:01.460
to. It's like who's really trying here?

00:30:01.660 --> 00:30:04.840
You know? Like is Capcom really giving us everything they could?

00:30:05.160 --> 00:30:13.300
I think that I'm more excited for all of these curiosities from the PC than I am from anything

00:30:13.300 --> 00:30:14.360
that comes from arcades.

00:30:14.420 --> 00:30:17.920
Just because I think that there's a lot more to explore there.

00:30:17.920 --> 00:30:21.240
So I am really hyped to play Pirates on the NES.

00:30:21.560 --> 00:30:24.500
The NES. It will come to the NES in 1991.

00:30:25.400 --> 00:30:26.340
Well, all right.

00:30:26.460 --> 00:30:27.520
Let's get ready. All right.

00:30:28.080 --> 00:30:30.540
And then there's another console out there.

00:30:30.540 --> 00:30:31.540
You might have heard of this one.

00:30:31.640 --> 00:30:33.400
It's called the Sega Master System.

00:30:33.760 --> 00:30:35.240
You know? I know of it.

00:30:35.400 --> 00:30:37.460
We don't really talk about it here on the show.

00:30:38.520 --> 00:30:42.780
Not much is known about the Sega Master System in our world because it didn't...

00:30:42.780 --> 00:30:46.940
It was in North America as early as 86 but it just didn't really sell well here.

00:30:46.940 --> 00:30:50.420
It did a lot better in Europe and shockingly in Brazil.

00:30:50.520 --> 00:30:51.660
It did really well.

00:30:52.240 --> 00:31:00.040
Yeah. I mean when I was a super NES guy and my friend had a Genesis.

00:31:00.340 --> 00:31:03.680
Like that's the only exposure I really had to Sega when I was a kid.

00:31:03.860 --> 00:31:10.260
And when I learned of the Master System before it, I didn't even know like what games were on it.

00:31:10.400 --> 00:31:12.060
But when you think of the Master System, I'm just wondering.

00:31:12.160 --> 00:31:14.040
This is just a test and it's okay if you don't know.

00:31:14.080 --> 00:31:16.540
What do you think of when you think of the Master System?

00:31:16.540 --> 00:31:20.300
I know of no game that is just associated with the Master System.

00:31:20.480 --> 00:31:26.560
Got it. And that's totally fine because absolutely the games I'm going to list out for you are strictly mostly arcade ports.

00:31:26.700 --> 00:31:27.840
Yeah. There's nothing here.

00:31:28.240 --> 00:31:32.340
Was Shadowrun a Master System or was that Genesis?

00:31:32.340 --> 00:31:37.540
It might have come out on the Master System but I think it would be a mess.

00:31:37.840 --> 00:31:42.280
Like even Sonic the Hedgehog actually did come out on the Master System after the Genesis.

00:31:42.660 --> 00:31:47.660
Wow. But like there was no real reason for it to exist and it is a bit of a different game.

00:31:47.900 --> 00:31:52.800
I was actually referring to the box art for Sega Master System games.

00:31:52.960 --> 00:31:53.840
They're all the same.

00:31:53.960 --> 00:31:57.520
They all have that like dot matrix like grid.

00:31:58.440 --> 00:31:59.960
Hold your horses.

00:32:00.540 --> 00:32:03.620
It's fine. Just type in like Sega Master System box art and you'll know what I'm talking about.

00:32:03.620 --> 00:32:10.100
It's just graph paper and then like a cartoon drawing of a character from the game.

00:32:10.260 --> 00:32:11.240
Oh this is gross.

00:32:11.280 --> 00:32:12.180
I hate it.

00:32:12.300 --> 00:32:15.360
I don't know why they thought that would be a good idea to make everything look uniform.

00:32:15.360 --> 00:32:17.140
It actually makes it look more boring.

00:32:17.400 --> 00:32:19.280
But even forget the box art for a minute.

00:32:19.480 --> 00:32:20.740
The games that are out.

00:32:21.080 --> 00:32:27.260
Okay. And I'm only doing up to our point in 1988 because I think that's important to think about all the games we played on the NES.

00:32:27.380 --> 00:32:33.700
And then here are the big hitters on the Master System and you tell me if Sega has a fighting chance against Nintendo right now.

00:32:34.500 --> 00:32:37.680
Fantasy Zone. Alex Kidd in Miracle World.

00:32:38.320 --> 00:32:39.280
Space Harrier.

00:32:39.880 --> 00:32:40.760
Wonder Boy.

00:32:41.600 --> 00:32:43.420
Outrun. And Afterburner.

00:32:45.460 --> 00:32:47.820
No. But you know they had Space Harrier.

00:32:48.060 --> 00:32:49.060
Space Harrier is cool.

00:32:49.500 --> 00:32:50.620
They had Outrun.

00:32:50.620 --> 00:32:51.880
Outrun is great.

00:32:52.060 --> 00:32:53.620
We talked about that almost more.

00:32:53.840 --> 00:32:55.340
Yeah. In our episodes.

00:32:55.840 --> 00:33:02.600
So they had a couple like cool things but no they don't have the library to compete right now.

00:33:02.640 --> 00:33:07.200
And I think even just being able to list off only six that are actually like even noteworthy.

00:33:07.420 --> 00:33:11.140
Yeah. Says something about the rest of these games.

00:33:11.480 --> 00:33:17.500
Exactly. And if it wasn't bad enough for Nintendo having that Master System competition.

00:33:17.980 --> 00:33:20.040
New competition has just entered Japan.

00:33:20.960 --> 00:33:25.860
But will never be a threat in North America for them which is where we focus our news on.

00:33:26.160 --> 00:33:29.000
But the PC Engine has just come to Japan.

00:33:29.260 --> 00:33:32.060
That's better known as the TurboGrafx-16.

00:33:32.600 --> 00:33:34.700
Do you have any familiarity with this console?

00:33:35.280 --> 00:33:36.820
I've heard of it.

00:33:36.960 --> 00:33:42.100
I remember when I would read about the different versions across platforms.

00:33:42.100 --> 00:33:47.440
Like I would see PC Engine and just thought that that meant it was a PC game.

00:33:47.940 --> 00:33:49.840
It does. It feels like that right?

00:33:50.060 --> 00:33:52.080
Yeah. But I guess not.

00:33:52.300 --> 00:33:55.180
I also have heard of TurboGrafx-16.

00:33:55.180 --> 00:33:56.400
But I know nothing about it.

00:33:56.480 --> 00:34:00.720
So the cheeky part about the PC Engine there's nothing misleading there.

00:34:01.160 --> 00:34:03.920
The TurboGrafx-16 implies what Sean?

00:34:03.920 --> 00:34:08.200
That it's so good looking but also 16-bit.

00:34:08.460 --> 00:34:10.540
Yes. And it is not 16-bit.

00:34:10.900 --> 00:34:13.480
It used an 8-bit CPU.

00:34:14.940 --> 00:34:16.140
And listed it twice.

00:34:16.720 --> 00:34:17.680
Right, right, right.

00:34:18.060 --> 00:34:22.160
And everything about the console is noticeably 8-bit.

00:34:22.520 --> 00:34:27.460
Okay. It had a larger display field than the NES.

00:34:27.460 --> 00:34:31.860
But that doesn't help it when essentially you're still only capable of making the same games.

00:34:32.500 --> 00:34:35.460
16-bit though was available in the arcades now.

00:34:35.600 --> 00:34:38.140
The arcades are mostly making 16-bit games.

00:34:38.560 --> 00:34:43.300
So we're going to start to see a lot of disparity between the quality of the ports.

00:34:43.520 --> 00:34:44.880
That's already been a thing.

00:34:45.000 --> 00:34:50.120
Right, right. But now like especially because you're dealing with a totally different type of system.

00:34:50.120 --> 00:34:53.060
You're just going to see basically total redesigns of the development.

00:34:53.400 --> 00:34:54.280
You know? Yeah.

00:34:54.280 --> 00:34:56.100
Just like oh we'll make the NES version of that.

00:34:56.320 --> 00:34:59.000
Yeah. It's sort of like those overlapping periods.

00:34:59.000 --> 00:35:01.060
At least with like Sony.

00:35:01.540 --> 00:35:06.040
When there would be games released on PS1, PS2 or PS2 and PS3.

00:35:06.280 --> 00:35:08.800
And they had to be completely different games.

00:35:09.260 --> 00:35:14.280
Yeah. Sometimes you would even see that with PS1 and N64 games.

00:35:14.560 --> 00:35:17.060
Like the N64 game would just be a different game.

00:35:20.880 --> 00:35:22.420
So let's bring it to Nintendo.

00:35:22.700 --> 00:35:23.860
Okay. All the way back.

00:35:23.980 --> 00:35:24.800
We're going full circle.

00:35:24.980 --> 00:35:37.100
Mm-hmm. Zelda 2, Castlevania 2, Final Fantasy, and Dragon Warrior or as known in Japan Dragon Quest are all out in Japan at this point in 1980.

00:35:37.100 --> 00:35:44.200
Do you think that like I feel like Zelda 2 is something that Americans are very aware about.

00:35:44.420 --> 00:35:51.920
Like that being out in Japan and they probably are hearing about it in different like trade magazines if you're into the video game scene.

00:35:52.320 --> 00:35:57.020
But do you think anyone actually has an appetite for Final Fantasy or Dragon Quest at this time?

00:35:57.220 --> 00:35:58.500
I don't think so.

00:35:58.620 --> 00:35:59.600
At least in North America.

00:35:59.940 --> 00:36:00.920
That's what we're talking about. Yeah.

00:36:01.120 --> 00:36:07.500
I don't think that the idea of a JRPG is really in anybody's head right now.

00:36:07.760 --> 00:36:13.620
Right. And if you are into RPGs, you're playing computer games completely.

00:36:14.180 --> 00:36:21.480
There's nothing on NES that's come close to being an RPG in the way that a computer game has been.

00:36:21.520 --> 00:36:25.260
Yeah. I imagine at least Ultima is out right now.

00:36:25.340 --> 00:36:28.720
Ultima is out. Yep. I think actually the first three Ultimas might be out right now.

00:36:29.640 --> 00:36:31.560
So that's what you're thinking of.

00:36:31.860 --> 00:36:38.820
And what Final Fantasy is going to supply is not even an RPG in that regard.

00:36:39.040 --> 00:36:45.940
So this is a completely new genre that we haven't seen yet for all intents and purposes.

00:36:46.200 --> 00:36:48.120
So I don't think anyone's really thinking about it.

00:36:48.340 --> 00:36:56.140
Yeah. And keeping in mind, too, that even though those were the games listed, there's some other notable ones, too, like Super Mario Brothers 2, which we just will never get.

00:36:56.440 --> 00:36:57.500
That's out in Japan, too.

00:36:57.500 --> 00:37:04.500
So it just kind of makes you think, like, you know, is Nintendo being too precious with the system right now?

00:37:04.680 --> 00:37:12.500
Are they are are what based on what we're looking at in terms of what's been released so far in 1988, knowing that these games do exist?

00:37:12.500 --> 00:37:18.180
Is it do you think it's more of a localization problem that they have to translate these games and they're figuring that out?

00:37:18.340 --> 00:37:23.080
Or do you think that they're just saying for for control of the quality of the product?

00:37:23.620 --> 00:37:26.060
If Zelda 2 gets released, Zelda 1 stops selling.

00:37:27.040 --> 00:37:37.000
I think that with their curation, especially time period, you know, we only had a crash however many years ago.

00:37:37.340 --> 00:37:39.080
It was 83. So five years ago.

00:37:39.220 --> 00:37:43.380
And so I can understand why they would be precious with it.

00:37:43.380 --> 00:37:47.240
Obviously, some garbage games have made it through this system.

00:37:47.380 --> 00:37:48.640
So it's not perfect.

00:37:50.000 --> 00:37:54.080
But to be fair, almost all those garbage games have been related to third party.

00:37:54.380 --> 00:37:57.280
We haven't really seen too many garbage games from Nintendo themselves.

00:37:57.560 --> 00:38:00.540
They're they're usually very carefully about what they put out.

00:38:00.540 --> 00:38:03.800
Yeah. And that's that's the essence of this question.

00:38:04.040 --> 00:38:12.040
And I think that because you just look at the difference between the NES and its competition right now, that it's night and day.

00:38:12.200 --> 00:38:23.720
And I think that has to do with, well, first party development and also curation of what else gets on the system and what it moves over to its secondary markets like North America.

00:38:23.980 --> 00:38:36.440
All right. And as we continue to do our chronological exploration of the Nintendo Entertainment System, what are you looking forward to in in on the NES that we haven't seen yet?

00:38:36.520 --> 00:38:45.140
Not game wise, but like maybe it's a game mechanic or a type of genre or a like something that you've already played.

00:38:45.260 --> 00:38:46.660
You want more of something like that.

00:38:46.660 --> 00:38:48.700
I think of something like Rygar for me.

00:38:48.820 --> 00:38:49.700
Like, I really like that.

00:38:49.820 --> 00:38:52.280
And I kind of want something that does that again, but better.

00:38:52.280 --> 00:38:56.720
Well, the I mean, we just said Final Fantasy.

00:38:56.980 --> 00:39:02.960
I'm I'm going to try that out because I actually have only played maybe an hour of it on the PlayStation port.

00:39:03.120 --> 00:39:06.160
OK, but beyond that.

00:39:06.860 --> 00:39:11.580
I have to go back to, you know, something like right on Bungling Bay.

00:39:11.780 --> 00:39:20.100
I want weird experiments again instead of like very tightly controlled genre affairs.

00:39:21.580 --> 00:39:23.920
So that's why I'm looking forward to pirates.

00:39:24.160 --> 00:39:28.260
I want to see more stuff that you can do on this system.

00:39:28.420 --> 00:39:45.140
Yeah. There seemed to be a period in mid 87 where we were getting a lot of and it was mostly coming from third party developers that were willing to take a chance on, you know, single development teams that were bringing out games like right on Bungling Bay or Spelunker.

00:39:45.140 --> 00:39:48.800
Yeah. Or even Jaws, even though we're a big fan of that game.

00:39:49.000 --> 00:39:57.020
Right. I think I think now we're kind of seeing because the console becoming more mainstream and it's selling a lot more units.

00:39:57.240 --> 00:39:59.720
You're seeing, well, this worked and sold really well.

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:01.040
It's consolidating this.

00:40:01.180 --> 00:40:07.440
I think the weird thing to that, and I put this on my list of things I'm looking forward to next, is if if people are paying attention to what's selling well.

00:40:07.560 --> 00:40:13.560
Right. We're seeing a lot of shmups and maybe that's because they're easy to program, but also because they're selling well and they're dominant in the arcade.

00:40:13.560 --> 00:40:23.640
Why aren't we seeing anything that tries to mimic Super Mario Brothers on an effective scale on like a game that plays just as good as Super Mario Brothers, but isn't Super Mario Brothers?

00:40:23.820 --> 00:40:27.940
I think that a lot of developers and again, I'm not a game developer, so I don't know.

00:40:28.380 --> 00:40:34.540
A lot of developers are trying to do Mario with this or Mario plus that.

00:40:34.700 --> 00:40:35.960
And that's what happens in you.

00:40:36.260 --> 00:40:38.920
That's what when you do that, you get Karnoff.

00:40:39.600 --> 00:40:51.640
Right. And while it definitely doesn't have the same feel like the successful ones, like Mega Man, I guess you can say, has lineages with Mario as well.

00:40:51.760 --> 00:40:58.840
So, I mean, that is something that was built off the back of the standard.

00:40:59.300 --> 00:41:00.740
Right. Okay. That's a good point.

00:41:01.260 --> 00:41:04.580
I didn't consider a game like Mega Man being in the Super Mario Brothers world.

00:41:05.960 --> 00:41:07.240
Not Super Mario world.

00:41:07.800 --> 00:41:09.600
Like a man in Super Mario world.

00:41:09.780 --> 00:41:17.940
And the last thing I'm looking forward to, you know, I'm just looking forward to what can dethrone our current shmup king and be the next shmup king.

00:41:18.120 --> 00:41:27.340
Like, it's funny that this genre that I feel like I had no attachments to before, I'm now thinking like, have we seen the peak?

00:41:27.720 --> 00:41:29.960
Or can it really get better on one console?

00:41:29.960 --> 00:41:41.120
Like, obviously, if I go play some games from some shmups from like the Super Nintendo or, you know, the PS1 or Sega Saturn, like those games are going to blow these NES ones probably away.

00:41:41.120 --> 00:41:48.720
But I feel like on this console, we still are seeing some progressive movements on these genres that we think are done.

00:41:49.200 --> 00:41:57.680
That being said, they will have to move mountains to get me to think that more can be done with shmups on this system.

00:41:57.680 --> 00:41:59.760
And I know there's still years to go.

00:41:59.860 --> 00:42:03.240
Mm-hmm. But I will be surprised if we find one better.

00:42:03.440 --> 00:42:15.680
So my last question then, are you worried that we're eventually going to regret doing this podcast as we enter a, like a unclear fog of the same over and over again?

00:42:15.780 --> 00:42:16.700
Do you think that will happen?

00:42:17.300 --> 00:42:19.720
I think we'll find a way to keep it interesting.

00:42:20.100 --> 00:42:22.700
I mean, how many games have we played so far?

00:42:22.760 --> 00:42:25.640
Like 150? We have not played 150.

00:42:25.700 --> 00:42:31.280
I wish. I'm pretty sure we've played 115 or something like that.

00:42:31.940 --> 00:42:33.320
It won't let me check right now.

00:42:33.380 --> 00:42:35.060
My finder is dead.

00:42:35.380 --> 00:42:36.280
So that's not good.

00:42:36.280 --> 00:42:43.760
We do find a way to talk about interesting things in games that we feel like we've already played.

00:42:44.020 --> 00:42:52.240
And maybe the volume that we have to look forward to of same-y games, like that's, it's disconcerting.

00:42:52.680 --> 00:42:55.220
But I still think it holds value.

00:42:55.220 --> 00:42:56.860
I think we'll be able to talk about it.

00:42:57.000 --> 00:43:03.140
As everyone who's been shouting at me as a listener probably knows right now, we are on episode 112.

00:43:03.140 --> 00:43:05.820
So we have played 112 video games.

00:43:06.420 --> 00:43:12.420
Unless you count, WorldCrest, TrackMe, and Stadium Events is the same game, in which case we've only played 111 unique games.

00:43:12.640 --> 00:43:15.160
However, I'd argue that's a really cool amount of video games.

00:43:15.280 --> 00:43:16.680
This project is really working out.

00:43:16.800 --> 00:43:23.220
And I hope that you guys at home who are listening and playing along, I hope you're playing all these games too, not just the ones that appeal to you.

00:43:23.220 --> 00:43:33.720
Because it shapes my perception of video games in a whole different way because I'm being exposed to games I never would have played.

00:43:33.940 --> 00:43:35.840
Oh, yeah. In both good and bad ways.

00:43:36.260 --> 00:43:43.360
And I think it forces you also to look at a game and try to actually think about like, well, they weren't trying to make total garbage.

00:43:43.360 --> 00:43:44.840
So what were they trying to do?

00:43:44.840 --> 00:43:49.120
Yeah, no, it sort of gets into art criticism.

00:43:49.400 --> 00:43:54.300
I mean, that's all we're doing is just art criticism at a very amateur level.

00:43:55.680 --> 00:43:57.560
But what is that game?

00:43:57.740 --> 00:44:02.260
It was one of the early episodes when we were still trying to like decide, at the side.

