Please recommend it to all of your friends, especially the ones that you think won't like this.

00:45:45.240 --> 00:45:47.860
Because that's important too.

00:45:48.220 --> 00:45:50.860
Somebody might hear it and be like, oh, I'm not interested in that.

00:45:50.860 --> 00:45:54.000
And then they go home and their wife is like, honey, what's wrong?

00:45:54.340 --> 00:45:56.180
And you're like, oh, you know, Scott from work.

00:45:56.180 --> 00:45:58.980
He offered to make me listen to this podcast called Nostalgia.

00:45:59.060 --> 00:46:00.080
And I don't like it.

00:46:00.300 --> 00:46:03.300
But secretly his wife has always liked Nostalgia.

00:46:03.500 --> 00:46:09.780
And Scott. So now she sees the connection and she goes and listens to Nostalgia.

00:46:10.000 --> 00:46:14.560
And to be honest, like if you have no exposure to things that you don't like

00:46:14.560 --> 00:46:18.280
and you only pick up things that you're pretty sure you're going to like,

00:46:18.280 --> 00:46:22.120
you have no baseline for what content can even be.

00:46:22.300 --> 00:46:27.760
That's why I watch things like, you know, Antiques Roadshow.

00:46:27.940 --> 00:46:30.060
I really don't care about antiques at all.

00:46:30.240 --> 00:46:34.260
But, you know, I just need to establish that baseline of what can entertain me.

00:46:34.380 --> 00:46:39.520
It's like why I avoid those books, you know, like a thousand and one places you must see

00:46:39.520 --> 00:46:42.160
or a thousand and one movies you have to see before you die.

00:46:42.320 --> 00:46:43.220
Don't curate my life.

00:46:43.240 --> 00:46:44.820
Books you have to read. It's like, I don't want to know.

00:46:45.480 --> 00:46:47.500
I want to read the really bad stuff too.

00:46:48.840 --> 00:46:50.940
And you're listening to the really bad stuff.

00:46:51.220 --> 00:46:52.940
So thank you so much.

